      "math_academy_id": "e-4155",
      "problem": "Evaluate $\\ln\\left(\\dfrac{1}{e^{3}}\\right).$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we rewrite $\\dfrac{1}{e^3}$ as a power of $e$ (the base of the natural logarithm) as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{e^3} = e^{-3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\ln\\left( \\dfrac{1}{e^3} \\right) = \\ln \\left( e^{-3} \\right).\n$$\n\nThe natural logarithm of a power of $e$ is the power itself:\n\n$$\n\\ln(e^x) = x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $x = -3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\ln\\left( e^{-3}\\right) = - 3.\n$$",
